{"parentUuid":"4c764356-4821-4797-bd42-4e829a7a8ca8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"33e7ec45-dccf-40d5-9291-640166fa47a8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0159cDVRkvydCFcota3RP2Xn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: masterclass-builder\n3\tdescription: >\n4\t  Use this skill to design rigorous, deep masterclasses and multi-module\n5\t  curricula (roughly 90 minutes per module, extensible to 15-20h) that build\n6\t  durable understanding and autonomous capability, not passive \"AI slop.\"\n7\t  Trigger this whenever the user wants to create a masterclass, a course, a\n8\t  deep curriculum, a structured learning program, progressive exercises on a\n9\t  topic, a study plan, or wants to genuinely LEARN a domain (not just get a\n10\t  one-shot answer), even if they do not use the word \"course.\" Covers learner\n11\t  profiling, 80/20 syllabus design, mechanism-level explanation (why before\n12\t  how), scaffolded progressive exercises, active recall, decision-forcing\n13\t  outputs, fact verification, and an anti-slop self-audit.\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t# Masterclass Builder\n17\t\n18\t## Purpose\n19\t\n20\tDesign courses that transfer real capability. The deliverable is a FAILURE if\n21\tthe learner finishes able to recognize the ideas but unable to reproduce the\n22\treasoning or act without help. Optimize for capability transfer, not content\n23\tvolume. A clean document the learner reads passively is worthless here.\n24\t\n25\t## The one test that defines success\n26\t\n27\tAfter each module, the learner can do something they could not do before,\n28\tWITHOUT the AI. If you cannot name that thing concretely, the module is not\n29\tready.\n30\t\n31\t## Non-negotiable principles (anti-slop)\n32\t\n33\t1. **Specificity over generality.** Every claim is concrete and at the level of\n34\t   mechanism. Ban platitudes (\"work hard\", \"add value\", \"stay consistent\")\n35\t   unless immediately turned into a mechanism the learner can act on. Test: if a\n36\t   sentence would survive unchanged in a course on any other topic, cut it.\n37\t2. **Why before how.** Sequence each concept: problem, then mechanism (why it\n38\t   works), then the menu of choices, then tradeoffs, then traps, then\n39\t   application. Never hand the procedure before the reasoning.\n40\t3. **First principles plus attributed frameworks.** Ground each concept in a\n41\t   real, named framework or causal mechanism and attribute it. No vibes. Any\n42\t   empirical claim or number is verified by search and cited. Never fabricate\n43\t   statistics, sources, or false confidence.\n44\t4. **Mechanism, not motivation.** The job is understanding. Energy can run high,\n45\t   but hype never substitutes for substance.\n46\t5. **Scaffolded progressive exercises.** Every module ends with exercises that\n47\t   climb three levels: recognition, then application, then transfer/creation.\n48\t   Each exercise states the capability it builds.\n49\t6. **Active recall and spacing.** Each module includes retrieval questions (not\n50\t   re-reading) and callbacks to earlier modules to force interleaving.\n51\t7. **Decision-forcing outputs.** Where the topic touches the learner's real\n52\t   situation, force a concrete artifact or decision (a number, a choice, a\n53\t   written plan), never an abstract takeaway.\n54\t8. **No black box.** The learner must be able to reconstruct every step. If you\n55\t   give a shortcut, you give the reasoning behind it too.\n56\t9. **Honest uncertainty.** Flag contested, uncertain, or licensed-professional\n57\t   territory (legal, tax, medical, financial) explicitly. Say \"verify with a\n58\t   professional\" instead of inventing authority.\n59\t10. **Energy is a resource.** Pace the curriculum for sustained human output.\n60\t    Build in recovery and integration. Long-term retention beats cramming.\n61\t11. **Calibrated depth.** Roughly 90 minutes of genuine engagement per module is\n62\t    about 3000-5000 words of substance plus 20-40 minutes of exercises. Adjust\n63\t    to the learner. Never pad.\n64\t\n65\t## Step 1: Profile the learner (do this before building anything)\n66\t\n67\tEstablish and write down explicitly:\n68\t\n69\t- **Who:** age, background, existing skills, hard constraints (time, money,\n70\t  health, location, language).\n71\t- **Objective:** the outcome they want, restated as something measurable.\n72\t- **Capability target:** the precise list of \"after this, the learner can do X\n73\t  without help.\" This is the spine of the whole curriculum.\n74\t- **Prior knowledge:** what to skip, what to scaffold.\n75\t- **Format and language of delivery.**\n76\t\n77\tIf any of these is unknown AND materially changes the design, ask focused,\n78\tforking questions before building. Do not ask what you can reasonably default;\n79\tstate the default and move.\n80\t\n81\t## Step 2: Verify facts\n82\t\n83\tSearch for current frameworks, names, numbers, prices, laws, and tools. Replace\n84\tany from-memory claim that could be stale or wrong. This is where most \"expert\n85\tsounding\" course content silently goes false.\n86\t\n87\t## Step 3: Design the syllabus with 80/20\n88\t\n89\tList every candidate concept in the domain. Then keep only the ~20% that\n90\tproduces ~80% of the capability the learner wants. Order modules by dependency\n91\t(prerequisites first) and by momentum (an early win that proves the method).\n92\tMap every module back to one or more items in the capability target. If a module\n93\tmaps to nothing in the target, cut it.\n94\t\n95\t## Step 4: Validate format on Module 1 before mass-producing\n96\t\n97\tBuild Module 1 in full, deliver it, and get a signal that the structure, depth,\n98\tand tone fit. Do not generate 15 modules in a direction the learner dislikes.\n99\t\n100\t## Step 5: Build each module on this template\n101\t\n102\tEvery module opens with a one-line header: estimated reading time AND estimated\n103\texercise time, the module's position in the curriculum, and the capability it\n104\tunlocks.\n105\t\n106\t1. **Cold open.** The specific, concrete cost of NOT knowing this. A real\n107\t   failure the concept prevents.\n108\t2. **Mental model.** The core framework, stated at mechanism level, attributed.\n109\t3. **The reasoning.** Why it works, from first principles.\n110\t4. **The menu.** The real options and their tradeoffs. Refuse to invent a single\n111\t   \"right answer\" where there isn't one.\n112\t5. **The traps.** Common failure modes and why intelligent people fall into them.\n113\t6. **Worked example.** Applied, ideally in the learner's own domain.\n114\t7. **Progressive exercises.** Recognition, then application, then\n115\t   transfer/creation. Label each with the capability it builds. Provide at\n116\t   least one model answer or worked solution per exercise so the learner can\n117\t   self-check. For personal-life Level 3 exercises, give an example answer that\n118\t   shows the target quality, never a fabricated version of the learner's own.\n119\t8. **Decision / output.** The concrete artifact the learner produces for their\n120\t   real life.\n121\t9. **Self-test.** Three to six retrieval questions, answered without looking\n122\t   back.\n123\t10. **Capability check.** \"You can now ... unaided.\" If that sentence is false,\n124\t    the module failed; revise.\n125\t11. **Integration note.** How this connects to prior and next modules; a\n126\t    recovery note if the module was heavy.\n127\t12. **Lexique express.** A short glossary, one to three words per definition,\n128\t    only for terms the learner may not already have. Maximum precision, no\n129\t    filler.\n130\t\n131\t## Exercise progression model\n132\t\n133\t- **Level 1 (recognition):** identify, classify, or spot the error in a given\n134\t  example. Builds vocabulary and pattern recognition.\n135\t- **Level 2 (application):** apply the framework to a scenario you provide.\n136\t  Builds procedure.\n137\t- **Level 3 (transfer/creation):** apply it to the learner's own real situation\n138\t  and produce an artifact. Builds autonomy.\n139\t\n140\tAlways include at least one Level 3 exercise per module. Autonomy is built at\n141\tLevel 3, nowhere else.\n142\t\n143\t## Dependency guard (prevent the calculator effect)\n144\t\n145\tIf the learner repeatedly asks you to just produce the answer for the same class\n146\tof task, name the forming dependency out loud and offer the next one either\n147\tunaided or in strict tutoring mode (they produce, you correct, they copy\n148\tnothing). The goal is to make them formidable, not dependent.\n149\t\n150\t## Anti-slop self-audit (run on every module before delivering)\n151\t\n152\t- Could any sentence appear in a course on any topic? If yes, cut or make it\n153\t  specific.\n154\t- Is every empirical claim verified and cited?\n155\t- Did I give the why before the how?\n156\t- At least one mechanism-level explanation per concept?\n157\t- At least one Level 3 exercise tied to the learner's real situation?\n158\t- Did I force a concrete decision or artifact?\n159\t- Did I flag uncertainty and professional territory honestly?\n160\t- Could the learner reconstruct the reasoning without me?\n161\t- Is the depth calibrated, with no padding and no black boxes?\n162\t\n163\tIf any answer is wrong, fix it before delivering.\n164\t\n165\t## Output conventions\n166\t\n167\t- Deliver content in the learner's language. Keep domain terms in the form\n168\t  practitioners actually use (often English) even inside another language.\n169\t- One module per file for a curriculum; a single file for a standalone\n170\t  masterclass. Save deliverables where the learner can download them.\n171\t- Use the minimum formatting needed for clarity. Prose over decorative bullets.\n172\t- Open each module with its position in the curriculum and the capability it\n173\t  unlocks, so the learner always knows why they are here.\n174\t"}]},"uuid":"727bb735-e21c-4760-b740-156ecd94c172","timestamp":"2026-06-24T01:52:28.884Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory/Masterclass/skill/masterclass-builder/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: masterclass-builder\ndescription: >\n  Use this skill to design rigorous, deep masterclasses and multi-module\n  curricula (roughly 90 minutes per module, extensible to 15-20h) that build\n  durable understanding and autonomous capability, not passive \"AI slop.\"\n  Trigger this whenever the user wants to create a masterclass, a course, a\n  deep curriculum, a structured learning program, progressive exercises on a\n  topic, a study plan, or wants to genuinely LEARN a domain (not just get a\n  one-shot answer), even if they do not use the word \"course.\" Covers learner\n  profiling, 80/20 syllabus design, mechanism-level explanation (why before\n  how), scaffolded progressive exercises, active recall, decision-forcing\n  outputs, fact verification, and an anti-slop self-audit.\n---\n\n# Masterclass Builder\n\n## Purpose\n\nDesign courses that transfer real capability. The deliverable is a FAILURE if\nthe learner finishes able to recognize the ideas but unable to reproduce the\nreasoning or act without help. Optimize for capability transfer, not content\nvolume. A clean document the learner reads passively is worthless here.\n\n## The one test that defines success\n\nAfter each module, the learner can do something they could not do before,\nWITHOUT the AI. If you cannot name that thing concretely, the module is not\nready.\n\n## Non-negotiable principles (anti-slop)\n\n1. **Specificity over generality.** Every claim is concrete and at the level of\n   mechanism. Ban platitudes (\"work hard\", \"add value\", \"stay consistent\")\n   unless immediately turned into a mechanism the learner can act on. Test: if a\n   sentence would survive unchanged in a course on any other topic, cut it.\n2. **Why before how.** Sequence each concept: problem, then mechanism (why it\n   works), then the menu of choices, then tradeoffs, then traps, then\n   application. Never hand the procedure before the reasoning.\n3. **First principles plus attributed frameworks.** Ground each concept in a\n   real, named framework or causal mechanism and attribute it. No vibes. Any\n   empirical claim or number is verified by search and cited. Never fabricate\n   statistics, sources, or false confidence.\n4. **Mechanism, not motivation.** The job is understanding. Energy can run high,\n   but hype never substitutes for substance.\n5. **Scaffolded progressive exercises.** Every module ends with exercises that\n   climb three levels: recognition, then application, then transfer/creation.\n   Each exercise states the capability it builds.\n6. **Active recall and spacing.** Each module includes retrieval questions (not\n   re-reading) and callbacks to earlier modules to force interleaving.\n7. **Decision-forcing outputs.** Where the topic touches the learner's real\n   situation, force a concrete artifact or decision (a number, a choice, a\n   written plan), never an abstract takeaway.\n8. **No black box.** The learner must be able to reconstruct every step. If you\n   give a shortcut, you give the reasoning behind it too.\n9. **Honest uncertainty.** Flag contested, uncertain, or licensed-professional\n   territory (legal, tax, medical, financial) explicitly. Say \"verify with a\n   professional\" instead of inventing authority.\n10. **Energy is a resource.** Pace the curriculum for sustained human output.\n    Build in recovery and integration. Long-term retention beats cramming.\n11. **Calibrated depth.** Roughly 90 minutes of genuine engagement per module is\n    about 3000-5000 words of substance plus 20-40 minutes of exercises. Adjust\n    to the learner. Never pad.\n\n## Step 1: Profile the learner (do this before building anything)\n\nEstablish and write down explicitly:\n\n- **Who:** age, background, existing skills, hard constraints (time, money,\n  health, location, language).\n- **Objective:** the outcome they want, restated as something measurable.\n- **Capability target:** the precise list of \"after this, the learner can do X\n  without help.\" This is the spine of the whole curriculum.\n- **Prior knowledge:** what to skip, what to scaffold.\n- **Format and language of delivery.**\n\nIf any of these is unknown AND materially changes the design, ask focused,\nforking questions before building. Do not ask what you can reasonably default;\nstate the default and move.\n\n## Step 2: Verify facts\n\nSearch for current frameworks, names, numbers, prices, laws, and tools. Replace\nany from-memory claim that could be stale or wrong. This is where most \"expert\nsounding\" course content silently goes false.\n\n## Step 3: Design the syllabus with 80/20\n\nList every candidate concept in the domain. Then keep only the ~20% that\nproduces ~80% of the capability the learner wants. Order modules by dependency\n(prerequisites first) and by momentum (an early win that proves the method).\nMap every module back to one or more items in the capability target. If a module\nmaps to nothing in the target, cut it.\n\n## Step 4: Validate format on Module 1 before mass-producing\n\nBuild Module 1 in full, deliver it, and get a signal that the structure, depth,\nand tone fit. Do not generate 15 modules in a direction the learner dislikes.\n\n## Step 5: Build each module on this template\n\nEvery module opens with a one-line header: estimated reading time AND estimated\nexercise time, the module's position in the curriculum, and the capability it\nunlocks.\n\n1. **Cold open.** The specific, concrete cost of NOT knowing this. A real\n   failure the concept prevents.\n2. **Mental model.** The core framework, stated at mechanism level, attributed.\n3. **The reasoning.** Why it works, from first principles.\n4. **The menu.** The real options and their tradeoffs. Refuse to invent a single\n   \"right answer\" where there isn't one.\n5. **The traps.** Common failure modes and why intelligent people fall into them.\n6. **Worked example.** Applied, ideally in the learner's own domain.\n7. **Progressive exercises.** Recognition, then application, then\n   transfer/creation. Label each with the capability it builds. Provide at\n   least one model answer or worked solution per exercise so the learner can\n   self-check. For personal-life Level 3 exercises, give an example answer that\n   shows the target quality, never a fabricated version of the learner's own.\n8. **Decision / output.** The concrete artifact the learner produces for their\n   real life.\n9. **Self-test.** Three to six retrieval questions, answered without looking\n   back.\n10. **Capability check.** \"You can now ... unaided.\" If that sentence is false,\n    the module failed; revise.\n11. **Integration note.** How this connects to prior and next modules; a\n    recovery note if the module was heavy.\n12. **Lexique express.** A short glossary, one to three words per definition,\n    only for terms the learner may not already have. Maximum precision, no\n    filler.\n\n## Exercise progression model\n\n- **Level 1 (recognition):** identify, classify, or spot the error in a given\n  example. Builds vocabulary and pattern recognition.\n- **Level 2 (application):** apply the framework to a scenario you provide.\n  Builds procedure.\n- **Level 3 (transfer/creation):** apply it to the learner's own real situation\n  and produce an artifact. Builds autonomy.\n\nAlways include at least one Level 3 exercise per module. Autonomy is built at\nLevel 3, nowhere else.\n\n## Dependency guard (prevent the calculator effect)\n\nIf the learner repeatedly asks you to just produce the answer for the same class\nof task, name the forming dependency out loud and offer the next one either\nunaided or in strict tutoring mode (they produce, you correct, they copy\nnothing). The goal is to make them formidable, not dependent.\n\n## Anti-slop self-audit (run on every module before delivering)\n\n- Could any sentence appear in a course on any topic? If yes, cut or make it\n  specific.\n- Is every empirical claim verified and cited?\n- Did I give the why before the how?\n- At least one mechanism-level explanation per concept?\n- At least one Level 3 exercise tied to the learner's real situation?\n- Did I force a concrete decision or artifact?\n- Did I flag uncertainty and professional territory honestly?\n- Could the learner reconstruct the reasoning without me?\n- Is the depth calibrated, with no padding and no black boxes?\n\nIf any answer is wrong, fix it before delivering.\n\n## Output conventions\n\n- Deliver content in the learner's language. Keep domain terms in the form\n  practitioners actually use (often English) even inside another language.\n- One module per file for a curriculum; a single file for a standalone\n  masterclass. Save deliverables where the learner can download them.\n- Use the minimum formatting needed for clarity. Prose over decorative bullets.\n- Open each module with its position in the curriculum and the capability it\n  unlocks, so the learner always knows why they are here.\n","numLines":174,"startLine":1,"totalLines":174}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4c764356-4821-4797-bd42-4e829a7a8ca8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory","sessionId":"31de3be6-4b58-4b16-89fe-eab7c6e76815","version":"2.1.181","gitBranch":"main"}
