hat P(A and B) = P(A)·P(B) holds ONLY for independent events. The synthesis is sound and standard, but it is a reasoned composition, not a single quotable authority — hence medium confidence and no verbatim quote. Do not attribute the exact phrasing to any one author.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Rarity is necessary but NOT sufficient for value: the market only pays for rare skill combinations it actually demands. The strongest peer-reviewed evidence shows the wage premium comes specifically from COMPLEMENTARITY — combining skills of DIFFERENT types — not from any arbitrary rare bundle. Stephany & Teutloff (Research Policy, 2024), analyzing 962 skills, find a skill's value is largely determined by how many high-value, different-type skills it complements; for example AI skills raise wages ~21% on average, driven by their strong complementarities.\",\n      \"attribution\": \"Fabian Stephany and Ole Teutloff, 'What is the price of a skill? The value of complementarity,' Research Policy, vol. 53 (2024); also arXiv:2210.01535\",\n      \"verbatimQuote\": \"\",\n      \"sources\": [\n        \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0048733323001828\",\n        \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.01535\",\n        \"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4234078\",\n        \"https://ideas.repec.org/a/eee/respol/v53y2024i1s0048733323001828.html\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"caveat\": \"This is the load-bearing 'market doesn't reward arbitrary intersections' source — peer-reviewed, large-N, directly on point. The 962-skills figure and the 21% AI-wage figure come from search summaries of the paper and the abstract; the central finding (value is driven by complementarity of different-type skills) is stated in the abstract and is high-confidence, but the precise numbers should be cited as reported-by-the-paper since I could not extract the full PDF text directly (the arXiv PDF failed to parse). Verify the two figures against the published abstract before quoting them as exact.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"There is a known selection-bias mechanism, Berkson's paradox, that makes the strategy look even better than it is in a specific way: among already-selected high performers (e.g. people who got hired or promoted on a combination of skills), two skills that are independent or even positively correlated in the general population can appear NEGATIVELY correlated. This means that within an elite pool the 'rare combination' can look rarer/more special than the underlying population justifies.\",\n      \"attribution\": \"Standard statistics literature on Berkson's paradox (Brilliant Math & Science Wiki; explainers by Eliott Kalfon and others)\",\n      \"verbatimQuote\": \"\",\n      \"sources\": [\n        \"https://brilliant.org/wiki/berksons-paradox/\",\n        \"https://eliottkalfon.com/posts/berkson-paradox/\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"caveat\": \"This is a more advanced, optional point. It cuts in a subtle direction (it explains why intersections can look special inside selected groups), and it is not specifically about skill stacking in the primary sources — it is a general statistical phenomenon I am applying to the case. Include only if the module wants depth; mark it as an applied analogy, not a documented critique of skill stacking per se. Hence medium confidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"An independent, well-known line of thought reinforces the constructive half of the model: Cal Newport's 'So Good They Can't Ignore You' argues that valuable, rare 'career capital' built through deliberate practice — not pre-existing passion — is what earns great work. This supports 'build rare+valuable skills' while saying nothing about multiplying percentiles, which is a useful way to separate the sound strategic core from the dubious arithmetic.\",\n      \"attribution\": \"Cal Newport, 'So Good They Can't Ignore You' (2012)\",\n      \"verbatimQuote\": \"\",\n      \"sources\": [\n        \"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/13525945-so-good-they-can-t-ignore-you\",\n        \"https://www.stafforini.com/docs/Newport%20-%20So%20good%20they%20can%27t%20ignore%20you.pdf\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"caveat\": \"Newport is adjacent, not a skill-stacking author; cite him only as corroboration that 'rare + valuable skills win' is mainstream, while explicitly noting he does not endorse (or even discuss) the percentile-multiplication calculation. Do not imply he validates the math.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"surprises\": [\n    \"The most credible version of the math (Pueyo's explicit 1,000,000 × 10%⁶ = 1) is also the one that openly states its own fatal assumption ('assumes skills are completely independent') — the popularizer is half-debunking himself, so the honest module should credit him, not strawman him.\",\n    \"Scott Adams, the person most credited with the idea, never actually multiplies percentages. The 'do the multiplication' move was added downstream. The Wealest summary even labels its one arithmetic-looking line 'Not actual math, but you get the idea' — the popular ecosystem already knows the math is loose.\",\n    \"The error direction is the opposite of what people assume. Because human abilities are POSITIVELY correlated (g-factor / positive manifold, SAT math–verbal ~0.61), the real 'both top 10%' group is BIGGER than 1% — the multiplication makes you feel RARER than you are, so it over-sells, not under-sells, your edge.\",\n    \"Pueyo's own GMAT anecdote (90th math + 95th verbal → 98th overall, not ~99.5th) is a live demonstration of the correlation flaw sitting right inside the article that promotes the formula — though part of that compression is also just the GMAT scoring formula, so it's a tell, not a proof.\",\n    \"There is peer-reviewed economics (Stephany & Teutloff, Research Policy 2024, 962 skills) showing the premium is real but ONLY for complementary skills of DIFFERENT types — which means the model's practical advice ('pick uncorrelated complementary skills') is vindicated by the same evidence that kills its naive arithmetic. The good advice and the bad math point in the same direction for different reasons.\",\n    \"I could not extract the arXiv PDF of the Stephany & Teutloff paper directly (it failed to parse), so the two specific figures (962 skills, +21% for AI skills) rest on search/abstract summaries and should be verified against the published abstract before being quoted as exact.\"\n  ]\n}","numLines":126,"startLine":1,"totalLines":126}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f3d3f339-c449-4af0-98df-b8c511edf171","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory","sessionId":"31de3be6-4b58-4b16-89fe-eab7c6e76815","version":"2.1.181","gitBranch":"main"}
